916: attempts/source_zero_normal_simple_guess. The successful
917: certificate keeps both coordinates in (SZ.33). It still leaves
918: the periodic real-normal clutch, the actual line multiplier and
919: the selected Tate-boundary attachment to be composed.
920: 
921: ## 17. The line multiplier in the normal-cone reset
922: 
923: The projective reset of Section 16 also has an exact retained line
924: coefficient. In the \((a,b)=(1,2)\) sector use
925: \(B=-2\lambda q\,U_1^2\), and let \(V_{\rm reg}\) be the regular
926: \(O(1)\) fibre coordinate on the \(z\)-chart of \(C_1\). Its relation
927: to the canonical meromorphic line coefficient is
928: \(V_{\rm reg}=z\,w_{\rm Cartier}\). Define
929: \[
930:  \widehat V=(1-w)V_{\rm reg},\qquad
931:  p=w\eta,\qquad r=\frac{B-D}{1-w}.
932: \]
933: Here \(w=(z-1)/(z+1)\) is the curve coordinate from Section 16;
934: it is not the meromorphic line coefficient.
935: The exact translation multiplier is \(-2\lambda q/(X_1X_2)\).
936: Taking its normal-cone limit only after multiplying the target
937: and source regular-frame factors gives
938: \[
939:  (w,p,r,\widehat V)\longmapsto
940:                   (pw,p,r,-2\lambda p\,\widehat V).         \tag{SZ.34}
941: \]
942: The base functions are retained:
943: \[
944:                        2v=r(1-p),\qquad T=-r(1+p).         \tag{SZ.35}
945: \]
946: The certificate verifies (SZ.34) from the full rational translation,
947: not from the projective map alone.
948: 
949: The node-divisor frame change in \(\widehat V\) is explicit. On the
950: nonzero \(O(1)\) line, use the two vector coordinates
951: \[
952:                        A=1/\widehat V,\qquad C=w/\widehat V .
953: \]
954: The reset is literally
955: \[
956:        (A,C)\longmapsto
957:        \bigl((-2\lambda p)^{-1}A,\;(-2\lambda)^{-1}C\bigr). \tag{SZ.36}
958: \]
959: Thus on a compact annulus of \(p\)-values the inverse period is a
960: diagonal Hopf contraction for sufficiently small \(\lambda\).
961: This names the actual normal-cone end, including its two
962: multipliers. The nodes \(p=0,\infty\) and the global periodic
963: attachment are still the charts from Sections 14--16; they have
964: not been deleted by this diagonal description.
965: 
966: ## 18. Rescaling the scalar with the source parameter
967: 
968: A direct fixed-\(\lambda\) central quotient retains the
969: zero-displacement period of Section 11. An alternative relative
970: comparison is to use the actual rescaling
971: \[
972:                           \lambda=v^N\lambda_0.
973: \]
974: On the two-node valuation ray, the source and suspension columns
975: then are
976: \[
977:                 h=(2,-a)^t,\qquad g=(b,N-1)^t,\qquad ab=2.
978:                                                                \tag{SZ.37}
979: \]
980: The source Tate parameter has order two, the source-line multiplier
981: has order \(-a\), the translated point has order \(b\), and the
982: selected Hom coefficient has order \(-1\) before this scalar
983: rescaling. These are exactly the valuations retained in
984: Sections 3, 6 and 10. The determinant is \(2N\).
985: 
986: For \(a=1,b=2\), use
987: \[
988:  U_N=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-2&1\end{pmatrix}
989:      \begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}
990:      \begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
991:  V_N=\begin{pmatrix}1&N-1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
992: \]
993: For \(a=2,b=1\), use
994: \[
995:  U_N=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix}
996:      \begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-(N-1)&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
997:  V_N=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}
998:      \begin{pmatrix}1&-2\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
999: \]
1000: All these matrices are unimodular and give
1001: \[
1002:  U_N\begin{pmatrix}2&b\\-a&N-1\end{pmatrix}V_N
1003:                          =\operatorname{diag}(1,2N).       \tag{SZ.38}
1004: \]
1005: Consequently the lattice quotient for \(N>0\) is
1006: \(\mathbb Z/(2N)\); for \(N=0\) it has the residual free
1007: \(\mathbb Z\). These are lattice statements. The finite quotient
1008: has not yet been identified with a collection of origin-link
1009: boundary components.
1010: 
1011: For the currently marked varying-source family, use the stronger
1012: choice \(N=M\), or a positive multiple of \(M\), and take the
1013: literal one-gamma parameter \(r=v^{N/M}r_0\). All finite-cap powers
1014: are then integral in \(v\). The rescaled loop contains \(N/M\)
1015: full one-gamma scalar turns in addition to the source turn. At
1016: the fixed comparison source these are \(bN/M\) of the proved
1017: shorter loops of P3K.35. No class in \(\Theta_7\) is divided.
1018: 
1019: Divisibility by \(M/b\) is the arithmetic lower requirement at a
1020: fixed source. It is not used here as a global varying-source
1021: endpoint theorem: the rational \(b\)-th root \(g_0(t,v)\) in
1022: P3K.35 must still be tracked around the source puncture before
1023: that smaller rescaling can be asserted. The actual finite-cap
1024: root maps and their source-parameter units remain part of the
1025: relative comparison.
1026: 
1027: The symbolic Smith identities and512 positive-\(N\) evaluations
1028: are saved in code/part3_fourfold/source_zero_rescaled_periods.py
1029: and its JSON/output. The remaining work is the compact relative
1030: periodic chart construction and its oriented boundary map.
1031: 
1032: ## 19. Exact Tate units on the two-node base
1033: 
1034: The period matrices used in Section 18 can be obtained from actual
1035: Tate coordinates on the full two-node base. The source has
1036: \[
1037:  c_4=16((1-2v)^2-3T).
1038: \]
1039: This is a unit at the origin. Its discriminant has the two simple
1040: factors \(q_1q_2\) of Section 10. Inverting the convergent \(j\)-series
1041: therefore gives
1042: \[
1043:  Q=q_T=q_1q_2u_Q,\qquad u_Q(0)=1/256.                 \tag{SZ.39}
1044: \]
1045: The indicated value follows directly from
1046: \[
1047:  j^{-1}=
1048:  \frac{(1-2v)^2T^2-4T^3-4(1-2v)^3v^2-27v^4+
1049:           18(1-2v)Tv^2}
1050:  {256((1-2v)^2-3T)^3}.
1051: \]
1052: All units in this section are holomorphic and nonvanishing on a
1053: smaller two-node polydisc; their logarithms are fixed there.
1054: 
1055: Here is the marked section map, including its lift rather than just
1056: its class in the Tate group. The convergent Tate Weierstrass sums
1057: extend over the node chart with coordinates \(Z_0,W_0\) and
1058: \(Q=Z_0W_0\). In these coordinates their first terms are
1059: \(x_T=Z_0+W_0+\text{higher terms}\) and
1060: \(y_T=-W_0+\text{higher terms}\). They consequently give the
1061: invertible analytic node map to the Weierstrass node, after the
1062: chosen \(c_4\)-root scaling and the Weierstrass translation. This is
1063: also seen directly by writing each negative-index summand as a
1064: power series in \(W_0\) and \(Q\); the positive-index summands are
1065: power series in \(Z_0\) and \(Q\).
1066: 
1067: The actual section \(G\) lifts through this node chart. Its two
1068: coordinates vanish on opposite discriminant branches. Choose the
1069: branch which, on the selected \(q_1=0\) normalization, represents
1070: the lift \(Q P_G\). The linear terms of the explicit Morse map in
1071: Section 10 are \(v+T/2\) and \(v-T/2\); hence those two vanishing
1072: orders are one. Analytic division gives
1073: \[
1074:  Z_0(G)=q_1u_G,\qquad W_0(G)=q_2u'_G,\qquad
1075:  P=P_G=\frac{Z_0(G)}Q=q_2^{-1}u_P.                   \tag{SZ.40}
1076: \]
1077: The branch is checked against the exact selected nodal value
1078: \((1+\sqrt{1+v})/(1-\sqrt{1+v})\). Since
1079: \(q_2|_{q_1=0}=-4v+O(v^2)\), it gives \(u_P(0)=16\).
1080: Thus (SZ.40) retains the actual section and the marked period
1081: lift, including its integral reindexing.
1082: 
1083: The line and its primitive translation have an equally direct
1084: formula. Set
1085: \[
1086:  f_P(Z)=\frac{\Theta(Z/P)}{\Theta(Z)}.
1087: \]
1088: The exact divisor and multiplier identify \(f_P\) with the
1089: canonical meromorphic section of the actual Cartier line. The
1090: function \(X\), whose divisor is \(G+(-G)-2O\), consequently has
1091: the literal expression
1092: \[
1093:  X(Z)=c_X f_P(Z)f_{P^{-1}}(Z).                       \tag{SZ.41}
1094: \]
1095: To see its scalar at the collision, evaluate at a fixed ordinary
1096: Tate point, for example \(Z=-1\). The identity
1097: \(\Theta(QZP)=-(ZP)^{-1}\Theta(ZP)\) shows that
1098: \(q_2 f_P(-1)f_{P^{-1}}(-1)\) is a nonzero holomorphic
1099: unit. The actual value \(X(-1)\) is a holomorphic unit there.
1100: Hence
1101: \[
1102:                          c_X=q_2u_X,\qquad u_X(0)=-1/4 .
1103:                                                                \tag{SZ.42}
1104: \]
1105: For the last value, the limiting source is
1106: \(Y^2=X^3+X^2\), so the retained scaling is
1107: \(X=4Z/(1-Z)^2\), and (SZ.41) gives \(c_X\sim-4/P\).
1108: No rational frame is discarded in this evaluation.
1109: 
1110: The target-point primitive lift of Section 6 is now exactly
1111: \[
1112:  U_a:(Z,V)\longmapsto(P^{-1}Z,c_X^{-a}V).             \tag{SZ.43}
1113: \]
1114: Indeed
1115: \[
1116:  f_P(Z)f_{P^{-1}}(Z/P)=1,
1117: \]
1118: so substituting (SZ.41) into the source and target Cartier
1119: coefficients cancels every \(Z\)-dependent factor. For \(a=2\)
1120: the literal map (SZ.16) is applied first. The resulting identity
1121: retains its central Cartier component.
1122: 
1123: Combining (SZ.18) and (SZ.43), the two actual periods are
1124: \[
1125:  \begin{split}
1126:  h:(Z,V)&\longmapsto
1127:        (QZ,P^aV),\\
1128:  g:(Z,V)&\longmapsto
1129:        (P^{-b}Z,\,
1130:         2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda_0v^Nq_1c_X^{-2}V).
1131:  \end{split}                                                   \tag{SZ.44}
1132: \]
1133: Their valuation matrices along \(q_1=0,q_2=0,v=0\) are precisely
1134: \[
1135:  I,\qquad W=\begin{pmatrix}1&b\\-a&-2\end{pmatrix},
1136:  \qquad E=NE_{22}.
1137: \]
1138: Every remaining factor in (SZ.44) extends as a unit. This proves
1139: the actual period/unit input to the sector fan construction; the
1140: earlier winding calculation alone was not used as that input.
1141: 
1142: ## 20. Residual periods on the two rank-one faces
1143: 
1144: The rank-one face actions retain nontrivial units. At the
1145: \(q_2=0\) face use the integral target character
1146: \[
1147:                          Y=Z^aV
1148: \]
1149: and the period basis \(h,\ k=gh^{-b}\). The kernel of \(W\)
1150: is generated by \((-b,1)\), and (SZ.44) gives exactly
1151: \[
1152:  \begin{split}
1153:   k:Z&\longmapsto (q_1u_Qu_P)^{-b}Z,\\
1154:   k:Y&\longmapsto
1155:    \frac{2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda_0v^N}
1156:         {q_1u_Q^2u_P^4u_X^2}\,Y.                    \tag{SZ.45}
1157:  \end{split}
1158: \]
1159: The other period has source multiplier \(Q\) and line multiplier
1160: \[
1161:                          c=(QP)^a=(q_1u_Qu_P)^a .
1162: \]
1163: Thus its actual central source is the nodal quotient of the Tate
1164: chain, with the degree-zero line glued by \(c\). The kernel map
1165: (SZ.45) acts on that line, including the source automorphism
1166: on the normalization. It has not been replaced by the identity.
